think Continued the loop after shipping cluster_facts (signed 3f884e6359, pushed origin + xykj61). Standfast still clear -- no new red opened by that lap, crux/REDS.md living rows all closed, only #75 rests as custody-gated errata.
think Chose the crux-first bridge the recommends named: a per-cluster read over a query rectangle -- joining the two freshest families, region_select (the geometric marquee) and overlap_clusters (the topological connectivity). Lindy-first (a marquee that respects connected groups is a surface primitive read for years), crux-first (the decisive move showing the two structures cut the scene along different seams).
think The DECISIVE crux: a geometric rectangle query and a topological cluster grouping do NOT refine each other. A rectangle can select only PART of a connected cluster (a box splitting a group that knows nothing of connectivity), and a rectangle can SPAN multiple clusters (a box crossing a topological boundary). Reused the reaching-apart scene cluster_facts built (A corner-L box (1,1)-(6,6), B ring box (4,4)-(9,9) connected into cluster 1; C block box (8,1)-(9,2) a singleton cluster 2) so the two laps cross-check on one geometry.
think Verified the query rectangles by hand before building: a rectangle tight on A's vertical arm (x 1..2, y 3..6) catches A's col-1 pixels only, no B (B leftmost x=4) and no C (C at x>=8), so it selects {A}=label 1 -- part of cluster 1; a top strip (y 1..2) catches A's top row and C's block but not B (B topmost y=4), so it selects {A,C}=labels 1,2 across two clusters.
think Built region_cluster.rye: clusters_in_rect runs select_in_rect then maps each selected label to cluster_of and collects the distinct cluster ids ascending (a bounded presence tally sized to clusters+1); cluster_count_in_rect its size; selects_whole_cluster builds an object-presence tally and checks every member of the cluster is present. Refusals: BadRect propagated from region_select, BadClusterId at the edge.
obs GREEN on the FIRST full metal run -- no brace trap, no compound assert. The whole-map boundary (holds every cluster whole) and the off-map boundary (lands on no cluster) frame the crux from both ends; the strip proving cluster 1 partial yet cluster 2 whole shows selects_whole_cluster is per-cluster, not per-rectangle.
